 The constant term in the expansion will be the term in the expansion of the binomial expansion where the powers of $x$ cancel each other out. This will occur for combinations of terms from $\left(10x^3\right)$ and $\left(-{\frac {1}{2x^{2}}}\right)$. 

Let's identify which combinations will give a constant term by setting the power of $x$ to 0 in the general term given by:
\begin{equation}
\binom{5}{r}\left(10x ^ 3 \right) ^ {5 - r}\left ( -\frac {1}{2x ^ 2} \right ) ^ {r}.\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
\binom{5}{r}\left(10 \right) ^ {5 - r}\left ( -1 \right) ^ {r} \left ( 2 \right) ^ {r}x ^ {3\left(5-r\right)-2r}.
\end{equation}
The term will be constant if $3\left(5-r\right)-2r = 0 \implies 15-5r-2r = 0 \implies r = 3.$
Thus the constant term is:
\begin{equation}
\binom{5}{3}10^2 \left(-\frac{1}{2^3} \right) = \frac{5*2*10^2}{2^3} = \frac{5 * 100}{8} = 62.5
\end{equation}
