Consider the point O and $A, B, C$. This is a symmetric arrangement, right? Then, $O$ would have to be the center of the circumsphere of $\triangle ABC, \ $ which means that $P, Q, A, B, C$ must all also lie on this sphere. Also, choosing the origin to be the circumcenter of $\triangle ABC$, the coordinates of $A, B, C$ are, by symmetry, $(0, 0, -R), \ $ $(R, 0, -R), \ $ $(0, R, -R), \ $ where $R$ is the circumradius. This gives us $P = (0, 0, R), \ $ $Q = (0, 0, R).$  Also, because $\triangle PAB \ $ must have a $120^{\circ}$ dihedral angle, $P$ and $Q$ must both be in front of $\triangle ABC, \ $ which determines our placement of them.


It follows that we must have $R = 200, \ $ which can be found by the Circumradius / Side Length / Circumradius / Side Length Relationship. So $O = (0, 0, 0).$ Now, let $(0, 0, R_1) \ $ be the coordinates of $P,$ which are equivalent to the coordinates $(-\sqrt{3}R, -R, R),$ by symmetry. Then, $2R^2 + R_1^2 + (\sqrt{3}R - R_1)^2 = 23 \cdot 600^2 \Rightarrow R_1 = (\sqrt{3} \pm 2)R = 200(\sqrt{3} \pm 2),$ which are $R_1 = 1200$ and $-1000 \ $ (notice that the second one is not possible). Go back to the original equations to find, by symmetry, that $O = (0, 0, 0)$ and $A = (0, 0, -200),$ so we find \begin{align*}
    d &= \sqrt{800^2 + 200^2} \\
        &= \boxed{821.565}.
\end{align*}
